Entry 34 — Paper 041 — Ephyra, Alperen Keles (Datadog): a Will-posted taking-in — the Datadog engineering blog post 'Ephyra: A New Agentic Property-Based Testing Library' (2026-11-24), posted bare in MOAT and ingested under the channel rule, read entire with its companion paper 'Ephyra: A Property-Based Testing Framework for the LLM Era' (arXiv:2611.23282v1 [cs.SE], submitted 23 October 2026, for the Future of Property-Based Testing workshop at POPL 27) read entire in HTML form beside it, and the public repository (datadog-labs/ephyra, MIT) read at its README and its Lean case-study index; outside the pre-registered sample, which closed at Paper 036, so no seal stands and no score is computed. The work: property-based testing's historical bottleneck is the generator, whose construction takes significant time and expertise, and Ephyra hands the generator to coding agents — the engineer states the property, the agent writes the generator and keeps rewriting it against measured coverage, steering its own in the closed loop — and the campaigns report hundreds of previously unknown bugs across the best-tested systems in open source, their lifetimes running from one day to twenty-four years. Integration founded a cluster rather than extending one: AU, the generator as the optimized artifact, argued at the founding against the sharpest claimant, AC, whose country is a search whose oracle is thin and whose knowledge lives in a hand-built workload, where this work's oracle is a stated property, complete as far as it goes, and the artifact under optimization is the generator program itself, rewritten inductively against measured coverage; AP (an artifact kept to reason with, against an instrument spent on another program's faults), AT (no trajectory; the reference is stated before the run), AK (one target's predicate is a format; the claims are about the loop) and B (the tests reshaped between runs, the system under test never) were weighed and held apart in the same scrolls. Fourteen concepts 1.2.561-1.2.574, one foundation (property-based testing, 1.3.370), no word coined, two stated state machines (the property run; the generator-optimization loop) bringing the held count to 200 (171 stated, 29 inferred), NI 0.0244, the axis judged no in writing — founding is not the axis — and no restructuring due: 574 stands below the 580 line set at Pass #25 and Paper 041 precedes Paper 045, the next trigger unchanged. The receipt-grade core was the recomputation check's fourth exercise, and its shape changed again: the first three exercises recomputed a paper's own tables; this one verified a work's claims against a public record outside the work. Four of the seven named upstream reports were read entire in their own trackers. The Lean issue (leanprover/lean4 16198, its tracker record read by API) states the mechanism exactly as the blog does — float literal patterns matched by the literal as written, not by its value — and carries its own sentence: 'The elaborated matcher is wrong, so #eval, compiled code and the kernel all agree on 0'; filed 2026-11-02, it stands closed on 2026-11-03, and the blog's 'fixed the day after we reported it' holds. The SQLite forum thread (9d039d4ca7) carries drh's same-day reply: the bug confirmed and root-caused on the record (a 3-byte minimal tuple rounded to 4 on a leaf, miscomputed as 8 rather than 7 as an interior divider, one byte of the page unaccounted), fixed by a check-in the same day, and his dating — 'the problem goes back until at least February 2014' — standing weaker than the blog's 'nearly 13 years' though consistent with it. The OCaml issue (15185, record read by API) shows the crash on both current branches and stands closed on 2026-10-28; the blog's '24 years' is not stated in the report and stands attributed. The PostgreSQL report (BUG #19758) gives the mechanism (the BETWEEN transform copying its left operand, twice per level, no interrupt checks in the parser), a 521-byte proof of concept at depth 20 and the doubling law; the house verified the law and the measured point (1.65 GB at depth 20), and the blog's larger case — 670 bytes, 26 levels, nearly 100 GB — follows from them (1.65 x 2^6 is about 105 GB) without being independently measured. The remaining three named reports — QED, LiteLLM, DuckDB — were not fetched, the fetch budget being spent; their existence is corroborated inside what was read, and they are filed on the blog's naming at 59.1.4. The counts were recomputed where a recount is a bounded act: the paper's arithmetic exact where it counts bugs (63 + 72 + 16 + 12 + 2 + 2 = 167 open-loop; Table 1's 30 + 17 + 14 + 11 + 8 = 80 closed-loop; the appendix's 167 + 80 + 72 + 30 = 349 decomposition), its Table 1 dollar figures carrying a one-dollar rounding grain — four of five row totals and two of three column totals off by exactly one from their printed cells, in alternating directions, the signature of totals computed from unrounded values, the $330 grand total consistent by both routes — and two repository indexes counted entire against the README's figures: 26 target systems, and the Lean index's 25 rows (13 fixed, 11 open, 1 reported by others), the Float-pattern bug among the fixed. The blog's headline totals — 535 found, 373 confirmed, 313 fixed across the 26 systems — were NOT recounted: the complete list lives in 26 per-target indexes, an exhaustive recount is not a bounded act at a polite pace, and the totals stand attributed beside the paper's October snapshot (349 found, 300 reported, fourteen systems), the difference filed at 59.1.4 as a caveat rather than a fault; a tracker that only recomputed would have certified the paper and missed the blog, and a tracker that only read the blog would have filed 535 on say-so. The Lean find is filed with its layer distinction stated precisely and guarded in both the check and the standoff (59.1.3, 59.2.1): a pattern-compilation correctness bug, upstream of evaluator and kernel, which each checked